1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:15,016
On the nineteenth of May 1536, inside the Tower of London, a queen of England was executed with a sword. Her husband, the king, had married her only three years earlier.

2
00:00:16,116 --> 00:00:27,615
Anne Boleyn had been convicted of adultery, incest and treason. Most historians believe the charges were false. Eleven days after her death, the king married again.

3
00:00:28,715 --> 00:00:38,016
Henry the Eighth married six times. Two marriages were annulled, two wives were beheaded, one died after giving birth, and one outlived him.

4
00:00:39,116 --> 00:00:48,460
He is remembered as a Renaissance prince who remade England's church, and as a tyrant who destroyed anyone in his way. Both are true.

5
00:00:50,660 --> 00:01:03,183
Henry was born at Greenwich in June 1491, the second son of Henry the Seventh, the first Tudor king, who had won his crown on a battlefield six years before.

6
00:01:04,283 --> 00:01:16,464
He was not meant to be king. But in 1502 his elder brother Arthur died, aged fifteen, months after marrying a Spanish princess, Catherine of Aragon.

7
00:01:17,564 --> 00:01:27,399
To keep the alliance with Spain, it was agreed that Henry would one day marry his brother's widow. The pope granted a special dispensation to allow it.

8
00:01:29,599 --> 00:01:43,743
In April 1509 his father died, and Henry became king at seventeen. Within weeks he married Catherine. He was tall, athletic, learned and musical, and foreign ambassadors praised him.

9
00:01:44,843 --> 00:01:53,205
He left much of the work of government to Thomas Wolsey, a butcher's son who rose to be a cardinal and the king's chief minister.

10
00:01:54,305 --> 00:02:07,767
He longed for glory and spent heavily on war with France. In 1513, while he campaigned there, his army at home crushed a Scottish invasion at Flodden, killing the Scottish king.

11
00:02:08,867 --> 00:02:19,512
In 1520 he met the King of France near Calais at a festival so lavish it became known as the Field of the Cloth of Gold.

12
00:02:20,612 --> 00:02:32,495
In 1521 he published a book attacking Martin Luther, and the pope rewarded him with the title Defender of the Faith. British monarchs still use it.

13
00:02:34,695 --> 00:02:44,871
Catherine was pregnant at least six times. Only one child survived infancy: a daughter, Mary, born in 1516.

14
00:02:45,971 --> 00:02:55,933
Henry was desperate for a son. His family's hold on the crown was only one generation old, and England had no tradition of a reigning queen.

15
00:02:57,033 --> 00:03:07,679
By 1526 he had fallen for Anne Boleyn, a sharp, well-educated woman at his court. She would not settle for being his mistress.

16
00:03:08,779 --> 00:03:19,104
Henry convinced himself his marriage was cursed, citing a verse in the Book of Leviticus against marrying a brother's wife. He asked the pope to annul it.

17
00:03:20,204 --> 00:03:32,577
The pope, Clement the Seventh, would not do it. He was under the power of Catherine's nephew, the Emperor Charles the Fifth, whose troops had sacked Rome in 1527.

18
00:03:33,677 --> 00:03:44,643
Wolsey failed to get the annulment. He was stripped of power in 1529 and died the next year, on his way to face charges of treason.

19
00:03:46,843 --> 00:03:55,312
A new chief minister, Thomas Cromwell, found a way out. If the pope would not grant an annulment, England would stop obeying the pope.

20
00:03:56,412 --> 00:04:11,153
Henry secretly married Anne in January 1533. His new Archbishop of Canterbury, Thomas Cranmer, declared the first marriage void. In September, Anne gave birth to a daughter, Elizabeth.

21
00:04:12,253 --> 00:04:21,235
In 1534, Parliament's Act of Supremacy declared the king Supreme Head of the Church of England.

22
00:04:22,335 --> 00:04:28,713
A new Treasons Act made it a capital crime even to call the king a heretic or a tyrant.

23
00:04:29,813 --> 00:04:44,299
Henry's former Lord Chancellor, the scholar Sir Thomas More, refused to swear the oath the new order demanded. He was executed in July 1535, weeks after Bishop John Fisher.

24
00:04:46,499 --> 00:05:02,051
Cromwell then turned to the Church's wealth. Between 1536 and 1540, more than eight hundred monasteries, nunneries and friaries were closed, and their lands taken by the crown.

25
00:05:03,151 --> 00:05:14,265
Thousands of monks, nuns and friars were turned out. Libraries, shrines and centuries of art were destroyed, and most of the land was sold to nobles and gentry.

26
00:05:15,365 --> 00:05:30,704
In 1536, tens of thousands in the north rose in protest, in the Pilgrimage of Grace. Henry promised pardons and a hearing. After further unrest, around two hundred people were executed.

27
00:05:31,804 --> 00:05:41,639
Catherine of Aragon, banished from court, died in January 1536, still insisting she was Henry's true wife.

28
00:05:42,739 --> 00:05:53,000
Anne, too, had failed to produce a living son. Within months she was accused of adultery with five men, one of them her own brother. All of them were executed.

29
00:05:54,100 --> 00:06:06,665
Henry married Jane Seymour eleven days after Anne's death. In October 1537 she gave him the son he wanted, Edward. Twelve days later, she died.

30
00:06:08,865 --> 00:06:19,511
His fourth marriage, to a German princess, Anne of Cleves, was annulled after six months. Henry blamed Cromwell, who had arranged it, and had him executed.

31
00:06:20,611 --> 00:06:28,589
That same day, Henry married Catherine Howard, still a teenager. Eighteen months later, she was executed for adultery.

32
00:06:29,689 --> 00:06:46,031
By now Henry was hugely overweight, with a painful ulcerated leg. A heavy fall while jousting in 1536 may have made it worse. Some historians think that fall changed him. Others say the cruelty was always there.

33
00:06:47,131 --> 00:06:52,827
His sixth wife, Catherine Parr, a widow and a published religious writer, outlived him.

34
00:06:55,027 --> 00:07:06,269
He spent his last years at war with France and Scotland. To pay for it, he sold off monastery lands and debased the coinage, mixing cheaper metal into the silver.

35
00:07:07,369 --> 00:07:18,847
He also built up the navy. In 1545 he watched from the shore as his warship, the Mary Rose, sank in the Solent with most of her crew.

36
00:07:19,947 --> 00:07:28,715
The killing went on to the end. Days before Henry died, the young poet Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey, was executed for treason.

37
00:07:29,815 --> 00:07:41,292
Henry died at Whitehall on the twenty-eighth of January 1547, aged fifty-five. His nine-year-old son became Edward the Sixth.

38
00:07:43,492 --> 00:07:58,468
All three of his surviving children reigned. Edward pushed England further towards Protestantism. Mary, Catherine's daughter, tried to restore Catholicism, and nearly three hundred Protestants were burned. Then came Elizabeth.

39
00:07:59,568 --> 00:08:12,859
Critics see a vain, cruel king who executed two wives, two of his closest ministers and hundreds of others, poured his father's fortune into wars, and destroyed centuries of monastic heritage.

40
00:08:13,959 --> 00:08:25,457
Defenders point to what lasted: a national church, a Parliament strengthened by the laws it passed for him, a larger navy, and the union of Wales with England in law.

41
00:08:26,557 --> 00:08:34,003
Henry tore England away from Rome, and married and discarded wife after wife, in his search for a male heir.

42
00:08:35,103 --> 00:08:44,319
His son died at fifteen. The daughter he had declared illegitimate, Elizabeth, became one of the most celebrated monarchs England ever had.
